  "id": "DrugBank_DB00520 - MONDO_44070",
  "verbalization": "Caspofungin, Anidulafungin, and Voriconazole all share the common mechanism of targeting fungal cell membranes through the echinocandin class of antifungals. This class of drugs inhibits fungal cell wall synthesis by interfering with the enzyme involved in the production of β-1,3-diketopiperazine, a key component of the fungal cell wall. The visible evidence suggests that Caspofungin, through its indication for Esophageal Candidiasis, may be repurposed to treat Candidemia, as it shares a common pathway with Anidulafungin and Voriconazole. The high scores in the final_score indicate a strong, consistent relationship across these three drugs, suggesting that the mechanism of action is a key driver of their therapeutic use in fungal infections. The evidence supports the hypothesis that Caspofungin, by targeting fungal cell wall synthesis, may be effective in treating Candidemia, with the potential for repurposing based on its established role in treating Esophageal Candidiasis.",
